A node id written inside an `if` is in a scope of its own, so it shadows an
outer binding of the same name as any nested declaration does -- and then has
to stop colliding with that binding in the *emitted* namespace, which is flat.

`#label` inside the `if` is what `label` means there. `var label` is what it
means outside. html emits a state var as `state.label` and a node handle by its
bare id, so before uniqueNodeIDs reserved the owner's own names the element
came out as `state.label`, and `state.label.textContent = "node"` wrote the
node's text through the var's cell. The id is the half that moves: a var's name
is read by everything the program wrote, while a handle is reached through its
binding.

The names are reserved from the package as well as from the owner. A
single-window program's state is hoisted onto the package by passHoistState
while its nodes stay on the window, so the var and the id that collide are
reached under two different owners -- and html emits both into one page
regardless.

`#tag` is the same question asked of two node ids rather than of a var and an
id. The inner one shadows the outer inside the `if`, uniqueNodeIDs renames it
for the namespace, and the read beside it has to follow that
rename -- an emitter spells a read from the ident's own name, so renaming the
node alone left one name for the element and another for every reference to it.
The handle is what links them, and passInlinePure was dropping it: it carried
`callsite.ID` onto the inlined override's node and left `callsite.Handle`
behind, which severed the two for every node whose component inlines -- which
is every stdlib component on every platform that overrides one.

The outer `#tag` is written first and so keeps the name; the inner one is the
copy that gives it up. Written the other way round the read needs no rename to
follow and the fixture asserts nothing.

deny out/none/html/index.html `state\.label\.` -- the element is not the var's cell
deny out/none/html/index.html `[^_]\btag\.value\b` -- the read follows the renamed node, not the name it was written as

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html {}
    }
}

ui.window #w(title="t") {
    var show = true
    var label = "outer"

    ui.vbox {
        ui.text #tag(value="outer tag")

        if show {
            ui.text #label(value="node")

            ui.text #tag(value="inner")

            ui.text(value="reads {tag.value}")
        }

        ui.text(value="{label}")
    }
}
